(window.DETAIL_C = window.DETAIL_C || {})["mth-12-15"] = {"mth-12-15-m01":{"idea":"A Bernoulli trial has two outcomes and a fixed chance of success.","steps":["The two outcomes are called success and failure, with probabilities p and q = 1 − p.","The chance p stays the same in every trial.","In a sequence of such trials, the trials are independent."],"others":"A trial whose success chance changes is not Bernoulli. Three outcomes or any number of outcomes do not fit the definition.","trap":"Two outcomes, constant p.","answer":"has exactly two outcomes, and the chance of success is the same each time"},"mth-12-15-m02":{"idea":"Drawing without replacement changes the chance each time.","steps":["After a heart is taken, the chance of another heart is 12/51.","After a card of another suit it is 13/51.","So the chance of success is not constant, and the draws are not independent."],"others":"Two outcomes alone are not enough. The number of suits and the count of hearts are not the reason. With replacement the draws would be Bernoulli trials.","trap":"Without replacement breaks independence.","answer":"not Bernoulli trials, as the chance of a heart changes after each draw"},"mth-12-15-m03":{"idea":"In B(n, p), n is the number of trials and p is the chance of success.","steps":["The number of trials n is fixed in advance.","Each trial has the same chance of success p.","So n and p are those two quantities."],"others":"The mean and variance are np and npq, not the parameters. The chance of failure is q. The number of successes is X itself.","trap":"n is a count, p is a chance.","answer":"the number of trials, and the chance of success in each trial"},"mth-12-15-m04":{"idea":"Count the ways, then multiply the probabilities.","steps":["Choose which x of the n trials are successes, in ⁿCₓ ways.","Each such sequence has probability pˣ for the successes and qⁿ⁻ˣ for the failures.","So P(X = x) = ⁿCₓ qⁿ⁻ˣ pˣ."],"others":"ⁿCₓ p q forgets the powers. The version with the powers swapped gives the chance of n − x successes. ⁿPₓ counts ordered arrangements, which is wrong here.","trap":"p goes with x, q with n − x.","answer":"P(X = x) = ⁿCₓ qⁿ⁻ˣ pˣ"},"mth-12-15-m05":{"idea":"The binomial probabilities are the terms of a binomial expansion.","steps":["(q + p)ⁿ = Σ ⁿCₓ qⁿ⁻ˣ pˣ.","The terms are exactly the probabilities P(X = x).","Since q + p = 1, the sum is 1ⁿ = 1."],"others":"A sum of n is impossible for probabilities. A product q × p gives a different expression. (q − p)ⁿ does not add up to 1.","trap":"q + p = 1.","answer":"(q + p)ⁿ, whose sum is 1"},"mth-12-15-m06":{"idea":"On average a fraction p of the trials succeed.","steps":["Out of n trials, about p of them succeed.","So E(X) = np.","For example, 100 tosses of a fair coin give a mean of 50 heads."],"others":"n + p adds a count and a chance. npq is the variance. n/p is far too large.","trap":"Mean is np, variance is npq.","answer":"np"},"mth-12-15-m07":{"idea":"Variance of a binomial is npq.","steps":["The variance is npq.","The standard deviation is √(npq).","Since q is less than 1, the variance is less than the mean np."],"others":"np is the mean. √(npq) is the standard deviation. n²pq is too large.","trap":"Do not take the root for the variance.","answer":"npq"},"mth-12-15-m08":{"idea":"Compare npq with np.","steps":["Variance = npq = (np) × q.","Since 0 < q < 1, the product is smaller than np.","So the variance is always less than the mean."],"others":"More than the mean would need q above 1. Equal would need q = 1. A large n does not change the factor q.","trap":"q is less than 1.","answer":"less than the mean, as q is less than 1"},"mth-12-15-m09":{"idea":"Use the complement: at least one is the opposite of none.","steps":["No success means all n trials fail, with probability qⁿ.","Subtract from 1.","P(at least one success) = 1 − qⁿ."],"others":"1 − nq is not the complement of qⁿ. qⁿ is the chance of none. 1 − pⁿ is the chance that not all trials succeed.","trap":"At least one means 1 minus none.","answer":"1 − qⁿ"},"mth-12-15-m10":{"idea":"All trials succeed: multiply p by itself n times.","steps":["Take the term x = n: ⁿCₙ q⁰ pⁿ.","That equals pⁿ.","For example, 5 heads in 5 tosses has probability (1/2)⁵ = 1/32."],"others":"np is the mean. nq is not a probability in general. qⁿ is the chance that all fail.","trap":"All success is pⁿ, all failure is qⁿ.","answer":"pⁿ"},"mth-12-15-m11":{"idea":"Use the binomial formula with n = 4 and x = 3.","steps":["P(X = 3) = ⁴C₃ (1/2)³ (1/2)¹.","⁴C₃ = 4 and (1/2)⁴ = 1/16.","So the probability is 4 × 1/16 = 1/4."],"others":"1/2 is too large. 3/8 is the chance of exactly 2 heads in 3 tosses, which is a different case. 3/16 forgets the number of sequences.","trap":"Four sequences, each 1/16.","answer":"1/4"},"mth-12-15-m12":{"idea":"Check the four conditions for a binomial.","steps":["Fixed number of trials: 10 throws.","Two outcomes: six or not six, with a constant chance 1/6.","The throws are independent, so the count of sixes is binomial."],"others":"Drawing without replacement breaks independence. The number of throws until the first six is not a fixed number of trials. Height is a measured quantity.","trap":"Fixed n, two outcomes, constant p, independence.","answer":"the number of sixes in 10 throws of a die"},"mth-12-15-m13":{"idea":"When p = q, the binomial distribution is symmetric.","steps":["With p = q, ⁿCₓ and ⁿCₙ₋ₓ are equal.","So the probabilities at x and n − x are the same.","The mean is n/2. It is not uniform, since ⁿCₓ changes with x."],"others":"Skew to the right or left needs p different from 1/2. A uniform distribution would need all probabilities equal.","trap":"Symmetric, but not flat.","answer":"symmetric, as P(X = x) = P(X = n − x)"},"mth-12-15-m14":{"idea":"Variance divided by mean gives q.","steps":["npq/np = q.","q = 3/4.","Then p = 1/4 and n = mean/p = 16."],"others":"3/16 mixes the numbers. 1/4 is the chance of success. 4/3 is the mean over the variance, which is above 1.","trap":"Variance over mean is q, not p.","answer":"3/4"},"mth-12-15-m15":{"idea":"Binomial probabilities peak near the mean.","steps":["The mean is np = 6 × 1/2 = 3.","The probabilities rise to a maximum near the mean and then fall.","They are 1, 6, 15, 20, 15, 6 and 1 in sixty-fourths, so the largest is 20/64 at x = 3."],"others":"p = 1/2 alone does not give the peak at 3. The variance does not locate the peak. n = 6 alone also does not.","trap":"Peak near the mean.","answer":"the mean is np = 3, and the binomial probabilities are the highest near the mean"},"mth-12-15-m16":{"idea":"Standard deviation is the root of npq.","steps":["npq = 10 × 0.3 × 0.7 = 2.1.","The standard deviation is √2.1.","√2.1 is about 1.45."],"others":"√0.21 uses a wrong product. 2.1 is the variance itself. √3 is the root of the mean.","trap":"Take the root of the variance.","answer":"√2.1, which is about 1.45"},"mth-12-15-m17":{"idea":"Leaving out one term reduces the total.","steps":["The terms for x = 0, 1, …, n add up to 1.","The term for x = 0 is qⁿ.","Without it the sum is 1 − qⁿ, so the statement is wrong."],"others":"The total of all probabilities is 1, but the student left out x = 0. Each term being a probability does not make a partial sum equal to 1. The sum is not qⁿ.","trap":"Do not forget x = 0.","answer":"wrong, as the sum is 1 − qⁿ, since the term for x = 0 is not included"},"mth-12-15-m18":{"idea":"n is the number of throws, p is the chance of a six.","steps":["There are 3 trials, so n = 3.","The chance of a six in each throw is 1/6.","So X follows B(3, 1/6)."],"others":"p = 1/2 is the chance of an even number. n = 6 confuses the number of sides with the number of throws. n = 1 counts one throw only.","trap":"n counts throws, not sides.","answer":"n = 3 and p = 1/6"},"mth-12-15-m19":{"idea":"Both mean and variance are multiples of n.","steps":["Mean np and variance npq.","With p and q fixed, both grow in proportion to n.","So increasing n increases both."],"others":"The mean cannot stay fixed when n grows. Neither decreases. The variance also grows.","trap":"Both are proportional to n.","answer":"increases, and so does the variance npq"},"mth-12-15-m20":{"idea":"With replacement, draws are independent.","steps":["The chance of a blue ball in each draw is 3/5.","All four draws must be blue.","The probability is (3/5)⁴ = 81/625."],"others":"(2/5)⁴ is the chance that all four are red. The product with a zero is not a valid way to count. 1 − (3/5)⁴ is the chance of at least one red.","trap":"None red means all blue.","answer":"(3/5)⁴ = 81/625"},"mth-12-15-n01":{"idea":"Use the binomial formula.","steps":["P(X = 2) = ⁵C₂ (0.3)² (0.7)³.","= 10 × 0.09 × 0.343.","= 0.3087."],"trap":"⁵C₂ = 10.","answer":"0.3087"},"mth-12-15-n02":{"idea":"Use the complement for at least one.","steps":["P(X ≥ 1) = 1 − P(X = 0).","P(X = 0) = (2/3)⁶ = 64/729.","So the answer is 665/729 = 0.9122."],"trap":"1 minus the chance of none.","answer":"0.9122"},"mth-12-15-n03":{"idea":"The mean of a binomial distribution is the number of trials times p.","steps":["The mean is E(X) = np.","Put in n = 20 and p = 0.25: 20 × 0.25.","So E(X) = 5.00."],"trap":"Multiply n by p.","answer":"5"},"mth-12-15-n04":{"idea":"Variance is npq.","steps":["First q = 1 − 0.25 = 0.75.","Var(X) = npq = 20 × 0.25 × 0.75.","So Var(X) = 3.75."],"trap":"Use q = 0.75.","answer":"3.75"},"mth-12-15-n05":{"idea":"Add the first three probabilities.","steps":["P(0) = (0.8)¹⁰ = 0.1074 and P(1) = 10(0.2)(0.8)⁹ = 0.2684.","P(2) = 45(0.2)²(0.8)⁸ = 0.3020.","Sum = 0.1074 + 0.2684 + 0.3020 = 0.6778."],"trap":"The count of ways for 2 successes in 10 is 45.","answer":"0.6778"},"mth-12-15-n06":{"idea":"Variance over mean gives q.","steps":["q = 4.5/6 = 0.75, so p = 0.25.","n = mean/p = 6/0.25.","So n = 24."],"trap":"Find p before n.","answer":"24"},"mth-12-15-n07":{"idea":"Variance over mean gives q, so p = 1 − q.","steps":["q = npq/np = 4.5/6 = 0.75.","p = 1 − q.","p = 0.25."],"trap":"p is one minus q.","answer":"0.25"},"mth-12-15-n08":{"idea":"Use B(4, 1/6) with x = 2.","steps":["P(X = 2) = ⁴C₂ (1/6)² (5/6)².","= 6 × 25/1296.","= 150/1296 = 0.1157."],"trap":"⁴C₂ = 6.","answer":"0.1157"},"mth-12-15-n09":{"idea":"The mode is the integer part of (n + 1)p.","steps":["(n + 1)p = 11 × 0.3 = 3.3.","Take the integer part.","The mode is 3."],"trap":"Check the neighbours if (n + 1)p is a whole number.","answer":"3"},"mth-12-15-n10":{"idea":"Use at least one = 1 − (5/6)ⁿ and solve the inequality.","steps":["We need 1 − (5/6)ⁿ > 0.9, so (5/6)ⁿ < 0.1.","n > log 0.1/log(5/6) = 12.63.","The least whole number is n = 13."],"trap":"Round up, not down.","answer":"13"},"mth-12-15-n11":{"idea":"Standard deviation is the root of npq.","steps":["Var(X) = 36 × 0.5 × 0.5 = 9.","The standard deviation is √9.","So it is 3.00."],"trap":"Root of the variance.","answer":"3"},"mth-12-15-n12":{"idea":"No defective item means every trial fails.","steps":["Each item is not defective with probability 0.95.","P(X = 0) = (0.95)¹⁰.","= 0.5987."],"trap":"Raise 0.95 to the power 10.","answer":"0.5987"}};
